akes me look like a jerk  yesterday he said I can no but you're  also showing people that this is real  people but the people would not be  silenced and nor would use of the mass  protests forced President Hosni Mubarak  to step down democratic elections  followed and Mohamed Morsi of the Muslim  Brotherhood was elected there was kind  of this state of unpredictability nobody  knew how far can we go in satire we were  placing bets which episode will be the  last for bassam's brand of satire was  getting him fans from outside Egypt he  soon earned the nickname the Egyptian  Jon Stewart ladies and gentlemen I also  just do it in Egypt which is something  that I'm very proud of thing is the  satire puts you like gets you into  trouble I mean what about the love we  get from the people I'll tell you this  it doesn't get me the kind of trouble  that gets you into if your regime is not  strong enough to handle the joke then  you don't have a regime  everybody wondered if democracy could  take root in Arabian soil it didn't take  long for the people to turn against the  Morsi government and his failed policies  protesters now started to demand morsi's  resignation and the military stepped in  Morsi may be a temptation to police by  the killer  let me finish today picketed General  Abdul Fattah al-sisi the army chief  deposed President Morsi it was a coup in  all but name  [Applause]  Yusuf's attempt to ridicule general  al-sisi were not tolerated by the regime  the state increasingly jammed his  broadcasts and threatened him with  lawsuits this time though the military  man had some popular support  it was unthinkable to even bring up the  name of CC in comedy I mean he was not  just there if not the room he was the  elephant in the room now you were  dealing not just for likely the dictator  or tyrant he had the people the mood  around Yusuf's show changed protestors  regularly gathered outside his studio he  was arrested on several occasions and  eventually the show was 